WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.940
How does a crest cell let go and find its way?

2
00:00:06.780 --> 00:00:08.260
And what happens when a step fails?

3
00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:11.820
To leave, a crest cell must loosen its grip.

4
00:00:12.660 --> 00:00:16.820
In chick, a cadherin called Cadherin-6B helps hold it to its neighbours.

5
00:00:17.983 --> 00:00:20.943
A transcription factor, Snail2, is needed.

6
00:00:21.903 --> 00:00:23.263
Block it, and fewer cells leave.

7
00:00:24.233 --> 00:00:27.693
Suppose Snail2 works by switching off Cadherin-6B.

8
00:00:28.773 --> 00:00:32.073
Lower Snail2. What happens to Cadherin-6B?

9
00:00:38.433 --> 00:00:43.153
Cadherin-6B RNA rises within about half an hour, just as predicted.

10
00:00:44.093 --> 00:00:47.073
The authors concluded Snail2 represses it directly.

11
00:00:48.167 --> 00:00:51.467
Lower Cadherin-6B instead, and cells leave early.

12
00:00:52.447 --> 00:00:56.287
It is a brake. Snail2 probably lifts it, at least partly.

13
00:00:57.400 --> 00:01:02.480
Now steering. In the chick trunk, crest cells pass only through the front half of

14
00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:03.480
each sclerotome.

15
00:01:04.333 --> 00:01:06.513
The back half repels them on contact.

16
00:01:07.413 --> 00:01:09.533
In chick, ephrin-B is one such signal.

17
00:01:10.533 --> 00:01:13.573
In mouse, semaphorin 3F also repels them.

18
00:01:14.473 --> 00:01:16.013
Neither is known to be the only cue.

19
00:01:17.033 --> 00:01:19.073
Crest cells also steer each other.

20
00:01:19.893 --> 00:01:23.533
When two touch, they collapse protrusions at the contact and move apart.

21
00:01:24.513 --> 00:01:25.713
Edge cells tend to move outward.

22
00:01:26.767 --> 00:01:31.167
Mutual attraction holds the group together, and a chemokine gives it direction.

23
00:01:32.267 --> 00:01:36.327
In frogs, a cluster follows that chemokine better than a lone cell.

24
00:01:37.167 --> 00:01:38.947
For trunk crest, this is less clear.

25
00:01:39.917 --> 00:01:44.157
Now the puzzle. The patient has Waardenburg syndrome type 4.

26
00:01:45.100 --> 00:01:49.820
The genes are SOX10, the endothelin receptor B and EDN3.

27
00:01:50.833 --> 00:01:56.193
Enteric precursors need them, and so do melanocytes, including those the inner ear

28
00:01:56.193 --> 00:01:57.193
needs for hearing.

29
00:01:58.017 --> 00:02:03.217
Try a toy model. One front moves down the gut from the mouth end, within a fixed

30
00:02:03.217 --> 00:02:06.917
time. Slow it. Which part stays empty?

31
00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:15.880
The end the front reaches last, the anal end.

32
00:02:16.700 --> 00:02:19.280
In the model, that segment is left without neurons.

33
00:02:20.383 --> 00:02:21.723
That is a toy model.

34
00:02:22.603 --> 00:02:26.323
In the clinic, too, the empty segment usually starts at the anal end.

35
00:02:27.283 --> 00:02:28.463
Rare cases skip a segment.

36
00:02:29.483 --> 00:02:32.623
An earlier failed step need not mean wider damage.

37
00:02:33.583 --> 00:02:36.023
Treacher Collins syndrome cuts crest numbers early.

38
00:02:36.903 --> 00:02:39.503
Yet it mainly affects the face, ears and eyelids.

39
00:02:40.417 --> 00:02:43.437
Tissues that fail together often share a requirement.

40
00:02:44.357 --> 00:02:48.697
Which tissues fail depends on where the gene is needed, the variant, and each

41
00:02:48.697 --> 00:02:49.697
tissue's sensitivity.

42
00:02:50.800 --> 00:02:52.740
One border, many destinations.

43
00:02:52.740 --> 00:02:56.700
The start biases the options, and the journey helps choose.
